{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Teaching in Tech-Rich Classrooms With TeachGen@i","title":"Module 2 Teaching Plan Studio","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting module 2 teaching plan studio to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"02","section":"08","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-02-08-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-02-08-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-02-08-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-02-08-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i","cat531_supplement_module_2_teaching_plan_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/02/07"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-02-08-lo-01":["cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_calibration","cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_ai_boundary"],"cat531-supplement-02-08-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_artifact_signal"],"cat531-supplement-02-08-lo-03":["cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_annotation_signal"],"cat531-supplement-02-08-lo-04":["cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_calibration"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Module 2 Teaching Plan Studio\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Module 2 Teaching Plan Studio, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/02-08-module-2-teaching-plan-studio.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Module 2 Teaching Plan Studio: Module 2 Teaching Plan Studio, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThis capstone asks you to fuse the module's separate skills (scenario setup, constraint prompting, suggestion comparison, learner-variability adaptation, and noticing) into one coherent teaching plan rather than a stack of disconnected pieces. The distinction that decides the grade is coherence versus stapling. A stapled plan places good components next to each other with nothing holding them together; a coherent plan subordinates every component to a single objective, so each move earns its place by serving that goal. The harder professional act is naming a real tradeoff: a 40-minute period cannot do everything a standard contains, and a defensible plan chooses depth on the right idea and says so, rather than pretending to cover it all. This section is about integration and principled choice under a fixed time budget.\n\nThe artifact is a Design Tension Studio map that shows one objective, the module's moves integrated around it, a defended tradeoff, and your documented AI judgments. A reviewer should see a plan that is unmistakably your professional work, not the tool's assembled output.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA candidate assembles the Module 2 capstone by pasting four things in sequence: the scenario he wrote, the three prompts he iterated, his adaptation list, and his noticing log. It looks complete; every required piece is present. But nothing refers to anything else. The scenario sets a 40-minute, no-device period, yet the adaptation list assumes a tablet app, and the noticing plan watches for an error the lesson never sets up. It is a folder, not a plan. The reviewer cannot tell what the lesson is for, because no single objective pulls the parts together. Completeness is not coherence; a plan is coherent only when removing any piece would visibly damage the goal.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA coherent plan is built around one objective that every component is accountable to, and it makes its tradeoffs explicit rather than hiding them.\n\n| Move from earlier sections | How it must serve the single objective |\n| --- | --- |\n| Scenario setup (02) | Fixes the binding constraints (40 minutes, no devices) the whole plan honors |\n| Constraint prompting (03) | Produces a plan that provably fits those constraints |\n| Suggestion comparison (04) | Keeps only activities whose cognitive demand matches the objective's verb |\n| Variability adaptation (05) | Gives every learner access to the same objective |\n| Noticing plan (06) | Watches for the specific error the objective is most likely to produce |\n\nIntegration means each move points back to the objective; if a component would survive unchanged no matter what the objective was, it is stapled, not integrated. The second demand is the tradeoff. A standard usually spans more than a single period can teach well, so the plan must choose: depth on the idea where the evidence shows reasoning breaks down, and an honest note that the rest moves to a later lesson. Naming the tradeoff (coverage versus depth) and defending the choice with evidence is the mark of professional judgment. Finally, because the plan was built with TeachGen@i, the map must keep your AI decisions visible (what you accepted, what you rejected, why), because a capstone is your work only when the tool's contributions are documented and bounded.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Here is the test for a stapled component: a noticing plan that watches for an error the lesson never sets up. Why does this reveal stapling rather than integration?\" explanation=\"An integrated component points back to the single objective; you could not swap it into a different lesson unchanged. A noticing plan that watches for an unrelated error would survive under any objective, which is the signature of a component pasted in beside the others rather than accountable to the goal.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The component would survive unchanged under any objective, so it is not accountable to this one\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is too detailed, so it belongs in a later lesson\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Noticing plans never belong in a capstone\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWalk through one annotated judgment call on assembling the Module 2 capstone. A candidate must turn the module's separate moves into one defensible 40-minute plan for a grade-7 lesson on comparing fractions.\n\n**Step 1. Reject stapling.**\n\n> **Before (rejected):** Four sections pasted in sequence: a scenario, three prompts, an adaptation list, and a noticing log, none referring to the others. Annotated: this is a folder, not a plan. Nothing shows the parts working toward one goal.\n\n**Step 2. Integrate around one objective.** Objective: \"Students explain why two fractions with the same denominator compare as they do.\" Each module move is tied back to it: the scenario fixes the 40-minute, no-device constraints; the constrained prompts produce a plan that fits them; the adaptation gives below-level readers an audio and visual route to the same explanation task; the noticing plan watches for the area-versus-comparison confusion seen earlier in the module.\n\n**Step 3. Name and defend the tradeoff.** Recorded on the map: \"Coverage vs. depth. The standard spans comparing, ordering, and equivalence, but 40 minutes only allows depth on one. I chose comparing same-denominator fractions because the module's exit-ticket data showed that is where reasoning broke down. Ordering and equivalence move to the next lesson.\"\n\n**Step 4. Keep the AI judgment visible.** The candidate logs one rejected TeachGen@i suggestion (a device-based fraction game that violated the no-device scenario constraint) with the reason. The final map shows one objective, integrated moves, a defended tradeoff, and documented AI decisions, which is what makes the capstone the candidate's professional work rather than the tool's output.\n\nMatch each module move to the way it is accountable to the single objective, explain why two fractions with the same denominator compare as they do. A move with no objective-serving sentence is stapled, not integrated.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Scenario setup (02)\",\"definition\":\"Fixes the 40-minute, no-device constraints the whole plan honors\"},{\"term\":\"Constraint prompting (03)\",\"definition\":\"Produces a plan that provably fits those constraints\"},{\"term\":\"Suggestion comparison (04)\",\"definition\":\"Keeps only activities whose cognitive demand matches the explain verb\"},{\"term\":\"Variability adaptation (05)\",\"definition\":\"Gives below-level readers an audio and visual route to the same explanation task\"},{\"term\":\"Noticing plan (06)\",\"definition\":\"Watches for the area-versus-comparison confusion the objective is most likely to produce\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nSubordinate every component to one objective and defend your coverage-versus-depth tradeoff with evidence. If a component would survive unchanged under a different objective, it is stapled and must be re-tied to the goal or cut.\n\nAudit the capstone for coherence before submitting:\n\n1. **One objective:** Can I state the single goal every component serves?\n2. **Accountability:** Does each move point back to that goal, or could it belong to any lesson?\n3. **Tradeoff:** Have I named what I chose not to cover and defended the choice with evidence?\n4. **AI visibility:** Are my accepted and rejected tool suggestions documented with reasons?\n\nA plan that is complete but not accountable to one objective is a folder, not a capstone.\n\nWalk the capstone assembly and watch each choice move the plan toward a coherent, defended whole or toward a folder of inert parts.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Assembling the Module 2 capstone\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"You have a scenario, three prompts, an adaptation list, and a noticing log for a 40-minute grade-7 fractions lesson. How do you assemble the capstone?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Paste the four pieces in sequence; every required part is present\",\"next\":\"staple\",\"feedback\":\"Completeness is not coherence. The pieces sit inert beside one another and nothing shows them serving one goal.\"},{\"label\":\"State one objective and tie every component back to it\",\"next\":\"objective\",\"feedback\":\"Right. A plan is coherent only when removing any piece would visibly damage the goal.\"}]},\"staple\":{\"prompt\":\"A reviewer notes the scenario says no devices but the adaptation list assumes a tablet app, and the noticing plan watches for an error the lesson never sets up. What is the diagnosis?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The components are stapled, not integrated; re-tie each to one objective\",\"next\":\"objective\",\"feedback\":\"Each component must point back to the goal or be cut.\"}]},\"objective\":{\"prompt\":\"Objective set: students explain why same-denominator fractions compare as they do. The grade-7 standard also covers ordering and equivalence, but 40 minutes cannot teach all three well. What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Touch all three lightly so the plan looks comprehensive\",\"next\":\"coverage\",\"feedback\":\"Spreading 40 minutes across three ideas means students master none. Refusing to choose is itself a failed decision.\"},{\"label\":\"Choose depth on comparing, where the exit-ticket data showed reasoning broke down, and move the rest to the next lesson\",\"next\":\"tradeoff\",\"feedback\":\"You named the coverage-versus-depth tradeoff and defended the depth choice with module evidence.\"}]},\"coverage\":{\"prompt\":\"Students leave shaky on all three sub-skills. How do you fix the plan?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Pick one sub-skill for depth and defend it with the exit-ticket pattern\",\"next\":\"tradeoff\",\"feedback\":\"Depth on the idea where reasoning breaks down, with evidence, is the professional choice.\"}]},\"tradeoff\":{\"prompt\":\"TeachGen@i proposes a device-based fraction game that would boost engagement. The scenario says no devices today. What do you log?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reject it on the map with the reason: it violates the no-device scenario constraint\",\"next\":\"done\",\"feedback\":\"You kept the AI judgment visible and bounded, so the capstone reads as your work.\"},{\"label\":\"Accept it; an undocumented tweak will not be noticed\",\"next\":\"coverage\",\"feedback\":\"An undocumented capstone cannot show whose judgment shaped it, and judgment is the competency under assessment.\"}]},\"done\":{\"prompt\":\"Your map shows one objective every component serves, a defended coverage-versus-depth tradeoff, and one documented rejected AI suggestion.\",\"feedback\":\"Coherence and a defended choice, not completeness, make the plan your professional work rather than the output of the tool.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nThe first misreading is to equate completeness with coherence: having every required section present is necessary but not sufficient, because the sections can sit inert beside one another. The second is to attempt full coverage in a single period, producing a plan that touches comparing, ordering, and equivalence so lightly that students master none; refusing to choose is itself a failed decision. The third is to leave the AI's contributions invisible, submitting a tool-generated plan as if it were entirely yours. An undocumented capstone cannot show whose judgment shaped it, and the competency under assessment is precisely your judgment.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nIntegrate a capstone in a different subject to see the structure rather than the fractions instance. A high-school biology candidate has a 50-minute period and the objective \"Students construct an evidence-based claim about why a population changed.\" The scenario fixes the constraints (50 minutes, a shared data set, no lab that day). The constrained prompt yields a plan that fits. Suggestion comparison rejects a flashy simulation that only asks students to watch, because the verb is construct a claim, not observe. The adaptation gives a multilingual learner a sentence frame for the claim while keeping the same reasoning demand. The noticing plan watches for the common error of citing data that does not support the claim. The tradeoff: the standard also covers mutation mechanisms, but the candidate spends the depth on claim construction because that is the assessed reasoning, and notes mechanisms for the next day. Same structure, new content, one accountable objective.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note (in linear terms: for each component of the plan, pair it with the sentence explaining how it serves the single objective): in the left column write each component; in the right column write its objective-serving sentence. If any right-column cell reads \"it does not, it is just included,\" that component is stapled. For the fractions capstone, every left-column move (scenario, prompts, adaptation, noticing) has a right-column sentence tying it to \"explain why same-denominator fractions compare as they do.\" A strong reader can also locate the tradeoff line and check that it is defended with evidence, not asserted. Your map is strong when no component floats free of the objective.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong capstone map states one objective and shows every component accountable to it, so a reviewer can see integration rather than assembly. It names a coverage-versus-depth tradeoff and defends the depth choice with evidence from the module (the exit-ticket pattern), not with preference. It keeps the AI decisions visible, with at least one rejected suggestion and its reason, marking the plan as the candidate's bounded use of the tool. Polish is a clean, well-formatted plan; evidence is the demonstrated coherence and the defended choice that show a teacher's judgment, not a tool's output.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the capstone from a single lesson to a week-long unit plan. The integration demand scales up: now five lessons must each be accountable to a unit objective, and the tradeoff becomes a sequencing decision about which sub-skills get a full day and which share one. The risk of stapling grows, because it is even easier to line up five separate good lessons that never build on each other. The same audit applies: one unit objective, each lesson accountable to it, a defended choice about depth across the week, and documented AI decisions throughout. If you can carry the coherence test from one period to a full unit without losing the single-objective spine, you have learned integration as a planning principle rather than as a way to organize one assignment.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A peer submits a capstone with all five required sections present and says it is complete, so it must be coherent. In two or three sentences, explain the difference between completeness and coherence using the two-column test, and describe the single move that would expose a stapled component.\" answer=\"Completeness means every required section is present; coherence means every section is accountable to one objective, so removing any piece would visibly damage the goal. The two can come apart: complete sections can sit inert beside one another. The exposing move is the two-column test: write each component on the left and the sentence saying how it serves the objective on the right; if any right-column cell reads it does not, it is just included, that component is stapled.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/02-08-module-2-teaching-plan-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"02.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 02.08. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_module_2_teaching_plan_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your capstone states one objective every component serves, defends a coverage-versus-depth tradeoff with module evidence, and documents your accepted and rejected AI suggestions. Coherence and a defended choice, not completeness, are what make the plan your professional work.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315886350\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_module_2_teaching_plan_studio","stem":"A capstone teaching plan has four labeled sections (scenario, prompts, adaptations, noticing) that never reference one another. Why does this fall short of an integrated plan?","options":["It is missing a fifth section.","The parts are stapled together; an integrated plan shows how each move serves one teaching goal.","The sections are in the wrong order.","It uses too few headings."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Integration means the parts reinforce one objective. A collated set of disconnected sections is assembly, not a coherent plan.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_assembly_not_integration","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i","cat531_supplement_module_2_teaching_plan_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-02-08-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_module_2_teaching_plan_studio","stem":"A candidate submits a flawless capstone plan generated entirely by TeachGen@i, with no teacher edits shown. Why is this a problem for a teaching portfolio?","options":["The plan is too detailed to be useful.","It shows no accepted, edited, or rejected AI input, so it does not document the candidate's own professional judgment.","AI plans are always lower quality than human ones.","The plan should have been longer."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A capstone must record where the candidate accepted, edited, and rejected AI input. Undocumented AI authorship is not evidence of the candidate's judgment.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_ai_authors_the_plan","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_module_2_teaching_plan_studio","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-02-08-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_module_2_teaching_plan_studio","stem":"The capstone plan must cover a broad standard in one 40-minute period. The candidate cannot do both full coverage and deep practice. What is the defensible move?","options":["Pretend the tension is not there and claim the plan does both.","Name the coverage-versus-depth tradeoff, choose depth on the highest-leverage subskill, and defend why with the standard and learner needs.","Let TeachGen@i decide and accept whatever it returns.","Cut the period short to avoid the choice."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A professional plan surfaces the tradeoff and defends the call. Hiding the hard choice is not the same as resolving it.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_no_defense_for_tradeoffs","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_module_2_teaching_plan_studio","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-02-08-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"Which Design Tension Studio map entry best captures an integrated, defensible capstone plan for a reviewer to audit?","options":["\"Combined all the module work into one document.\"","One objective stated, each module move (scenario constraints, constrained prompts, an access-preserving adaptation, a noticing-driven revision) tied back to that objective, plus a named coverage-versus-depth tradeoff with the defended choice and one rejected AI suggestion.","\"Used AI to write the whole plan.\"","\"Plan covers everything in the module.\""],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strongest capstone map shows one objective, integration of each module move toward it, a defended tradeoff, and documented AI judgment, all of which a reviewer can inspect.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_assembly_not_integration","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_module_2_teaching_plan_studio","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-02-08-lo-01","cat531-supplement-02-08-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_assembly_not_integration","pattern":"stapling_mistaken_for_integration","description":"Treating the capstone plan as a folder that staples together each module piece rather than a coherent plan where the parts reinforce one objective","trigger":"candidate submits scenario, prompts, adaptations, and noticing as disconnected sections with no through-line","feedback":"Show how each module move serves one teaching goal. A coherent plan is integrated, not collated.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_ai_authors_the_plan","pattern":"ai_authorship_in_capstone","description":"Letting TeachGen@i generate the whole capstone plan and presenting it as the candidate's professional judgment","trigger":"the plan shows no teacher edits, no rejected suggestions, and no decisions the candidate can defend as their own","feedback":"The plan must record where you accepted, edited, and rejected AI input. Undocumented AI authorship is not your judgment.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_2_8_module_2_teaching_plan_studio_no_defense_for_tradeoffs","pattern":"tradeoffs_left_undefended","description":"Making the plan's competing demands disappear instead of naming the tradeoff and defending the choice","trigger":"candidate hides a tension such as coverage vs. depth rather than stating which they chose and why","feedback":"Surface each tradeoff and defend the call. A plan that pretends there were no hard choices is not yet professional.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"cf874007edb73b21b2e1d7acf82cfaf5370b9388f407e3633578b4d1b671f558","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}